What you’re reacting to is less “rules” than asymmetric moralization.

People often take a preference, convention, or coordination mechanism and quietly upgrade it into a virtue. Then the inverse gets treated like a defect. “Early riser” becomes disciplined; “night owl” becomes lazy. “Rule-following” becomes responsible; “rule-bending” becomes reckless. But in many cases those are not opposites in any deep moral sense—they’re different operating styles under different constraints.

The clean move is to separate four things that people constantly collapse together:

\text{rule} \neq \text{reason for rule} \neq \text{outcome} \neq \text{character}

A rule can be arbitrary in origin yet useful in practice. It can be sensible for most people and unnecessary for someone with more skill or context. It can be outdated. It can exist purely to coordinate strangers. And violating it can be harmless, foolish, creative, negligent, or necessary depending on the actual circumstances. The label alone does not settle the question.

Your irritation makes sense when somebody says, effectively, “My frame is neutral; yours requires justification.” That is the hidden asymmetry.

The useful counter is not “all rules are fake, therefore none matter.” It’s:

Show me the function. Show me the harm. Show me the constraint. Show me why this frame deserves privilege over the alternative.

If they can answer that, now you’re talking about reality instead of etiquette dressed up as morality.

And the hubris check is simple: being able to see that a rule is contingent does not automatically mean you’ve correctly modeled every consequence of breaking it. So keep the freedom, but pair it with consequence-tracking. That’s stronger than obedience and stronger than rebellion.

A good shorthand for the whole thing is:

\text{judge consequences more than conformity}

That preserves the frame shift without pretending every frame is equally good in every context.